Hi, I’ll play the “Andrew” role in this conversation and say that I don’t recommend this sort of weighting of likelihood factors, as it moves you away from a generative model.  To put it another way, if lambda really is constant, it does not make sense to downweight old observations; and if lambda varies, you want to model that, to have lambda(t) which varies over time.  Such a model will make more sense and have the effect of downweighting old observations.
A

> On Dec 2, 2014, at 9:59 AM, Ken Butler <nxs...@gmail.com> wrote:
>

> I have some data that go back into the past, and I would like to downweight old observations, say an exponentially weighted scheme that weights year-old observations half as much as current ones. Is there a way of handling that in Stan that has escaped my attention (likely!) As an example, say I have data that i believe to be Poisson with constant mean lambda, each observation having a date attached, but I would like to give more weight to recent observations as above?
>

