Thanks Ben, what you said make sense to me. I understand it is ideal to estimate the random effect for each marker from a Bayesian perspective.

However, given the fact that plugging in the random effect mode decrease the sampling time from 1 hour to 1 min for each marker. I am still stuck with this idea. What if it is legitimate to assume the parameter, in our case the random effect, is independent with the new information in the second model, which is the marker? In another word, there is no new information to flow back for a better estimate of the random effect. In terms of ignoring the parameter distribution, can I argue it is actually better in the sense that by taking the random effect mode, it will affect each of my markers equivalently, instead of differently when estimate it every time?

With some reading, the plug in idea seems concord with the empirical bayes method, would you agree on this?

On Thursday, December 4, 2014 8:55:15 PM UTC-5, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> It ignores the uncertainty in the distribution of the parameter. And it does not allow the information in the second model to flow back into the posterior distribution of the parameter. Basically, it is the same reason why using the cut() function in BUGS is not Bayesian.

