Thanks Bob,

The log density issue has already been taken care of.

However, when running the optimizing function, I occasionally came across this error message: Error evaluating model log probability: Non-finite gradient. Please see file attached illustrating the optimization process.

There is a big jump after this error message appears. Log probability before this jump was already in the right range but suddenly jumped to a wrong value. Also, the optimization process can go through smoothly with reasonable result if I put another run for the same data. Do we have a method to get around this?

I used default parameters for the optimizing function: fit1 <- optimizing(model_glm, verbose = FALSE, data = dataList)

Thanks much,

Xulong

Update: Using algorithm BFGS, instead of the default LBFGS, is giving me consistent reasonable results on a small test data set. But BFGS is slower.

Update: Actually same problem happens with BFGS on a bigger data set.

On Wednesday, December 10, 2014 3:35:47 PM UTC-5, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> The main problem is that you can't fit hierarchical models with
> optimization unless you are very careful with priors (see Gelman et al.'s
> papers on this topic).
>
> The second problem is that we only compute the log density up to a constant
> normalizing term, and it includes the prior as well as the likelihood,
> so you can't directly compare log likelihoods this way.  You could compute
> the log likelihood in your model if you want, say in generated quantities.
> If you use the functional forms (e.g. normal_log(y,mu,sigma) instead of
> the sampling notation form y ~ normal(mu,sigma)), we include all the normalizing
> terms.
>
> - Bob
>
>
