Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Ben,

The trick you provided significantly decreased the sampling time. I here attached 2 versions of my stan models. To remind you, this is an ordered categorical model with a random effect defined by known covariance matrix. Here are the main difference between the two attached files: in glmNull1.stan: u = multi_normal(0, sigmaU * CK); in glmNull2.stan: u = sigmaU * (CK * z), with z the primitive random distribution as you described.

While the first model took about 17 hours with N = 600 and a 600 by 600 covariance matrix, the second only took about 1 hour.

However, the results are also very different. Posterior distributions of U from the two models are without the range of comparison! There are essentially in different scales.

More importantly, It seems the MCMC sampling method on both models are giving me wrong estimations on the random effects and log joint posterior density. While testing on simulation data, the optimizing() method from Stan returned reasonable results, but the sampling() not. Again, posterior distribution of u from the sampling method was very hard to believe, in the sense that both mode and variation are very high.

This description might be too general for you to decode the problem. But please take a look at the Stan code first, and let me know what other information you want to see.

Thanks much,

Xulong

On Monday, October 27, 2014 1:00:56 PM UTC-4, Ben Goodrich wrote:

> On Monday, October 27, 2014 11:31:16 AM UTC-4, Xulong Wang wrote:
> > I am building an ordered categorical model with a random effect. The model can be written as: f(y) ~ x * beta + u, where u ~ (0, sigma * K), and K as a covariance matrix is known. The model as attached below is running fine but very slow. It takes hours for a 2000 iteration run. Could you please give me your suggestions to speed things up?
>
> Yes, see edits to your model below. Basically, it comes down to the rule to not use any of the variants of multi_normal() unless the left-hand side is data.
>
> > Secondly, in a separate model with an additional predictor, is it possible to reuse the estimation of the random effect u? I have to include thousands of genetic variants, means there will be thousands of run. So, speed and efficiency is important to me.
>
> If I understand you correctly, you cannot do so in a theoretically satisfying way. This is similar to the discussion of the cut() function in the BUGS family of software.
>
> > //--- The model
> > data {
> >   int<lower=1> N;  // Sample number
> >   int<lower=1> K;  // Response categories
> >   matrix[N,N] KS;  // Covariate matrix
> >   int<lower=1,upper=K> Ad[N];  // Response
> >   int<lower=1,upper=2> Sex[N];  //  M:F
> > }
> >
> > transformed data {
> >   matrix[N,N] CK;  // Cholesky K
> >   # vector[N] mu;  // 0 (not needed anymore)
> >   vector[N] sexF;  // Relative to M
> >
> >   CK <- cholesky_decompose(KS);
> >
> >   for (i in 1:N) {
> >     # mu[i] <- 0; // not needed anymore
> >     sexF[i]  <- Sex[i]  == 2;
> >   }
> > }
> >
> > parameters {
> >   #vector[N] u;  // random effect
>       vector[N] z; // primative of random effect
> >   real<lower=0> sigmaU;
> >
> >   real pSex;  // Female
> >
> >   positive_ordered[K-1] cut;  // cut points
> > }
> > transformed data {
>       vector[N] u;
>       u <- sigmaU * (CK * z);
>     }
> > model {
> >   vector[N] yhat;
> >
> >   pSex ~ normal(0, 5);
> >   sigmaU ~ normal(0, 5);
> >
> >   #u ~ multi_normal_cholesky(mu, sigmaU * CK);
>       z <- normal(0,1); // implies u ~ multi_normal()
> >
> >   yhat <- u + sexF * pSex;
> >
> >   for (n in 1:N)
> >     Ad[n] ~ ordered_logistic(yhat[n], cut);
> > }
> >

