From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Mon Dec 22 19:58:24 2014
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Mon, 22 Dec 2014 19:58:24 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] How to convert from real to integer?
Message-ID: <mUJ-NLkmEdQ.xzvGgVIjvrcJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <mUJ-NLkmEdQ.ql89ZlzL0BkJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <mUJ-NLkmEdQ.vDbyToq8yYoJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <mUJ-NLkmEdQ.ql89ZlzL0BkJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/mUJ-NLkmEdQ/m/xzvGgVIjvrcJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

By design, there's no way to convert a real to an integer in Stan.
The problem is that it loses gradient information and we were too
worried people would use it to create models that wouldn't work.

For your application, you can switch to using a while loop.
Instead of:

  for (n in 1:floor(50/0.01))
    ...

which is illegal, you can do:

  {
    int n;
    n <- 1;
    while (n < floor(50/0.01)) {
      ...
      n <- n + 1;
    }
  }

But I wouldn't recommend implementing Euler given that we have a
Runge-Kutta implementation built in.  It's imprecise to autodiff
an iterative algorithm (no error control), and Euler's a lousy
integration algorithm (steps must be too small to maintain error
bounds due to order of error).

- Bob

